    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. can you dig a bit deeper into the leucine threshold? like, is there a specific amount needed per meal to actually flip that mTOR switch effectively? and also, how much surplus are we talking for nitrogen balance—are we looking at a slight edge or a heavy surplus to keep it anabolic?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The leucine threshold is quite specific, and research suggests that consuming at least 2-3 grams of leucine per meal is required to effectively activate the mTOR pathway. This concentration is often referred to as the \"optimal leucine dose.\" Consuming even slightly lower amounts (e.g., 1-1.5g per meal) may still have some activation effects, but the response may be blunted.\n\nAs for nitrogen balance, a moderate caloric surplus of 250-500 calories above the maintenance level is often sufficient to promote an anabolic state. This translates to a daily caloric intake that is 10-25% above maintenance levels. A slightly higher surplus (500-750 calories) can further enhance nitrogen retention and promote more significant muscle growth, but be careful not to overdo it, as excessive caloric intake can lead to insulin resistance and muscle wasting.\n\nIt's also worth noting that the anabolic window, when nutrient uptake and absorption occur, is typically around 1-2 hours after exercise. Consuming a post-workout meal or snack with 20-30g of protein, along with 5-10g of leucine, can help promote protein synthesis and nitrogen balance within",
      "source": "target",
